---
title: Introduction
description: Ergosfare v1 — a modular, reflection-light mediator for CQRS-style messaging in .NET.
---

Ergosfare is a mediator for CQRS-style messaging in .NET: commands, queries and events over
one pipeline model, with interceptors for cross-cutting concerns and covariant handler
matching.

{% aside type="caution" %}
**You are reading the v1 documentation, and it is deliberately brief.**

Active development happens on the **v2 preview line**, which is where the interesting work
is: compile-time registration through a source generator, a `ValueTask`-first surface,
pooled execution contexts, working Native AOT support, and nested dispatch scopes. v2 is
close to stable.

If you are starting something new, **start on [v2](/ergosfare.docs/preview/getting-started/introduction)**.
If you are already on v1, the [migration guide](/ergosfare.docs/preview/migration/v1-to-v2)
is short.
{% /aside %}

## What v1 gives you

- **Modular.** Commands, Queries and Events are independent packages over a shared core.
  Register only the modules you use.
- **Interceptor pipeline.** Pre, post, exception and final stages for logging, validation,
  authorization and cleanup, attached without touching handlers.
- **Covariant interceptor matching.** An interceptor registered against a base type or
  interface applies to every assignable message.
- **DI-native.** First-class `Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection` support; handlers
  resolve from the calling scope.
- **MIT licensed**, no enforced external dependencies.

## What it looks like

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Commands.Abstractions;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Core.Abstractions;

public sealed record CreateProduct(string Name) : ICommand<Guid>;

public sealed class CreateProductHandler : ICommandHandler<CreateProduct, Guid>
{
    public Task<Guid> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, IExecutionContext context)
        => Task.FromResult(Guid.NewGuid());
}
```

Registration scans an assembly at startup:

```csharp
using System.Reflection;

builder.Services.AddErgosfare(o => o
    .AddCommandModule(c => c.RegisterFromAssembly(Assembly.GetExecutingAssembly()))
    .AddQueryModule(q => q.RegisterFromAssembly(Assembly.GetExecutingAssembly()))
    .AddEventModule(e => e.RegisterFromAssembly(Assembly.GetExecutingAssembly())));
```

## How v1 and v2 differ

The v2 line is not a rewrite of the concepts — messages, handlers and interceptors work the
same way. What changed is how registration and dispatch are implemented:

| | v1 (this line) | v2 preview |
|---|---|---|
| Registration | Runtime assembly scan | Source generator, at compile time |
| Handler return | `Task` / `Task<T>` | `ValueTask` / `ValueTask<T>` |
| Execution context | Parameter, plus `AmbientExecutionContext` | Parameter only; pooled |
| Nested dispatch | Ambient scope | `context.CreateScope()`, isolated |
| Native AOT | Not supported | Supported, with dispatch roots |
| Event handlers | Exact event type only | Covariant, like interceptors |
| Allocation (100k dispatches) | — | ~2.3 MB |

